COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — contains nothing but an empty stretch of cracked grey asphalt and flat shallow lagoon water running toward the camera, dark and free of detail so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, sign and key object sits in the upper five-eighths. Truck cabs, railings and the roadway itself run across the MIDDLE of the frame, never near the bottom edge, and anything resting on them sits at mid height. Frame people from the chest up and place their heads high, close to the top edge. Photographed from a low road embankment beside a causeway across a shallow lagoon in southern Crimea, camera at standing eye level in flat late-afternoon light under a high overcast sky. On the left half a wide four-lane concrete road bridge lies broken: one span has dropped into the shallow water, bent reinforcing bar sticking out of the torn concrete, the roadway on top completely empty. On the right half, only a few metres away, a narrow floating pontoon crossing barely wider than one vehicle carries a single file of heavy cargo trucks stopped bumper to bumper, tarpaulin sides sagging, engines off, one driver's door hanging open. The contradiction sits in one frame: the big road is intact-looking but dead, every truck is squeezed onto a floating strip. A Russian long-haul driver in his fifties, in a faded grey work jacket, stands on the running board of his cab on the upper right, framed from the chest up, one hand shading his eyes as he looks down the stalled line, mouth slightly open, shoulders dropped, sweat at his temples. Observed detail: a hand-written cardboard queue number wedged behind his windscreen wiper, curling at the edge. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/4, standing eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained colour, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, steel, concrete and water. Signs may clip a little but the overall image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare, no explosions, no sparks, no smoke particles floating in the air. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no logo; Cyrillic lettering on road signs and truck doors inside the scene is allowed only as small, partly out-of-focus texture and does not need to be legible.

